  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThe marine layer pushed in efficiently this morning, filling in \nthe coastal basin and western valleys. High clouds associated with\nan upper level low over Central CA will continue to stream into \nSoCal through the afternoon. Even as the low marine layer clouds \nburn off, these higher clouds will keep temperatures a bit cooler \ntoday as well as decreasing heights thanks to the aforementioned \nlow. Highs this afternoon will be 2-6 degrees below normal, in the\nmid 70s for coastal areas, 80s for inland spots and High Desert, \nand 90s for the lower deserts. The cooling trend continues into \nMonday as the low deepens to the north, encouraging stronger \nsouthwest flow and a deeper marine layer both Monday and Tuesday. \nWhile moisture isn't robust, both mornings may feature a deep \nenough marine layer to be sufficient for some localized patchy \ndrizzle. \n\nThe low will become absorbed into the larger trough and sweep \nnorthward on Tuesday. Behind it, CA will be in transition between a \nstrengthening high over Texas and a large Canadian low north of \nWashington. The Texas high will attempt to elongate and return the \nregion to at least weak ridging, but influence from the Canadian low \nmay limit the heating towards the end of the week. For now, high \ntemperatures should return to near normal or slightly above, \nespecially for Thursday/Friday as the ridge becomes compressed by \nthe low propagating eastward along the Canadian/US border. This \ncould shift the thermal axis closer to CA and increase \ntemperatures on Thursday around 10-15 degrees compared to Monday's\ncooler temps. The spread remains wide on high temps later this \nweek, but confidence is growing on a backdoor trough behind the \nmain Canadian low that will stretch down to CA sometime over the \nweekend, likely cooling temperatures back to normal by Sunday as \nthe ridge is pushed east. \n\nModels are beginning to show the development of a tropical cyclone \nsouth of Baja late this week. NHC indicates that there is about a \n90% chance of development here in the next 48 hours, although there \nis no confidence on track until after formation. Early indications \nsuggest should something form, the elongated high may influence the \npropagation of such a system, and we will keep a close eye on any \ndevelopment here or if there are any impacts associated. Further \ninformation can be found at NHC's website at nhc.noaa.gov.",
